// unscii-16 as a lookup, generated.
//
// **Not rewritten and elided rather than omitted.** `packages/raster/tools/genfont.ts` writes
// `packages/raster/src/font16.wac` from a vendored `.hex` file — 3,240 glyphs, 13,932 words — so the
// file is a generator's output and the interesting decisions are in the generator. The three
// functions below are its whole surface and are what `surface.wac` imports.
//
// One thing in it is worth carrying across, because it is a fact about the host rather than about
// the font. The shipped README:
//
// > **The glyph table is split across two constant arrays**, and that is not tidiness: V8 refuses
// > `array.new_fixed` above 10,000 elements, and the font is 13,932 words. A single `const i32[]`
// > does not compile — *"Requested length 13932 for array.new_fixed too large"* — which is worth
// > knowing before writing any other large table in wac.
//
// So a source-level layout decision is made by an engine limit, and `glyphRow` below is the function
// that hides it. Nothing in `spec/` mentions a ceiling on a constant array; a program that grows one
// past 10,000 entries finds out from V8. `../../../QUESTIONS.md` has it as a question about where a
// host limit belongs, since `--host wasmtime` may well not have this one and the split is in the
// source either way.

/** Where `cp` is in the table, or `-1`. See the note in `surface.wac` on the sentinel. */
export i32 glyphIndex(i32 cp) { … }

/** Row `row` of glyph `idx`, as a bit pattern `glyphWidth(idx)` bits wide. */
export i32 glyphRow(i32 idx, i32 row) { … }

/** 8, or 16 for the points unscii draws double. */
export i32 glyphWidth(i32 idx) { … }
